WEBVTT

1
00:00.400 --> 00:03.530
This video is for the person who has to approve it.

2
00:04.000 --> 00:08.450
Someone wants an AI assistant to read Db2 for i.

3
00:08.560 --> 00:10.650
Four questions come first.

4
00:10.720 --> 00:12.250
What can the model do?

5
00:12.560 --> 00:14.670
Whose authority does it use?

6
00:14.720 --> 00:16.390
Where do the rows go?

7
00:16.440 --> 00:19.130
And what is written down afterwards?

8
00:20.430 --> 00:24.520
Start from the worst case, and treat the model as untrusted.

9
00:24.910 --> 00:31.500
A prompt injection in an email, a document or a table row can make it ask for anything.

10
00:31.550 --> 00:34.760
So nothing here depends on the model behaving.

11
00:34.830 --> 00:38.140
The server enforces the policy on every call,

12
00:38.190 --> 00:41.800
and the IBM i decides what each user may read.

13
00:42.030 --> 00:44.680
The model only sends requests.

14
00:45.980 --> 00:48.490
First, whose authority.

15
00:48.540 --> 00:51.050
There is no shared service profile.

16
00:51.100 --> 00:55.370
Each person signs in with their own IBM i user profile,

17
00:55.420 --> 00:58.810
on a sign-in page the server serves itself.

18
00:58.860 --> 01:02.170
The server tests the password with a real connection,

19
01:02.220 --> 01:05.210
and every query runs as that user.

20
01:05.260 --> 01:09.823
Object authority and exit programs apply as they always have.

21
01:09.873 --> 01:17.350
Each token refresh tests the profile again, so a disabled profile is locked out at the next refresh.

22
01:18.650 --> 01:21.380
Next, what the model can do.

23
01:21.530 --> 01:26.280
The tools are the attack surface, so keep only the ones you need.

24
01:26.330 --> 01:30.760
Allow and deny lists decide which tools exist at all.

25
01:30.810 --> 01:35.320
Turn off execute_query, and free-form SQL is gone,

26
01:35.370 --> 01:36.600
not hidden.

27
01:36.650 --> 01:39.240
What remains can be business tools:

28
01:39.290 --> 01:43.400
vetted queries your team wrote in YAML, with bound parameters,

29
01:43.450 --> 01:48.020
so a value from the model is never pasted into the SQL.

30
01:49.320 --> 01:54.350
Free-form SQL is read-only in three independent layers.

31
01:54.400 --> 01:58.470
The validator parses each statement and scans its text:

32
01:58.520 --> 02:03.110
one statement, starting with SELECT, WITH or VALUES.

33
02:03.160 --> 02:05.670
No data change hidden in a FINAL TABLE,

34
02:05.720 --> 02:08.550
and no QCMDEXC.

35
02:08.600 --> 02:12.230
The connection itself is opened read-only by the driver.

36
02:12.280 --> 02:14.790
And IBM i parses the statement,

37
02:14.840 --> 02:16.870
and must call it a query.

38
02:16.920 --> 02:19.190
One exception, stated plainly:

39
02:19.240 --> 02:23.537
object DDL and statement cancel use a second connection,

40
02:23.587 --> 02:26.450
which runs only fixed statements.

41
02:28.350 --> 02:32.380
Then, what it can read, and where it could send it.

42
02:32.430 --> 02:37.260
IBM i services that reach outside the database are refused:

43
02:37.310 --> 02:41.180
HTTP calls, IFS writes and email.

44
02:41.230 --> 02:50.220
The library allowlist checks every table, view and qualified function against the names that IBM i's own parser returns,

45
02:50.270 --> 02:53.927
including subqueries and common table expressions.

46
02:53.977 --> 03:00.360
An unqualified table function is refused too, because it resolves through the SQL path.

47
03:02.260 --> 03:07.210
Column masking hides values such as email and phone from the agent.

48
03:07.260 --> 03:10.450
A mask only helps if it can't be bypassed,

49
03:10.500 --> 03:14.450
so a masked column may only be selected as itself.

50
03:14.500 --> 03:19.517
An alias, a function, a filter or a union is refused.

51
03:19.567 --> 03:21.730
But be clear about what this is.

52
03:21.780 --> 03:27.410
It changes what the agent receives, not what the user can read with another tool.

53
03:27.460 --> 03:32.590
Row and column access control in Db2 is the control that holds.

54
03:34.490 --> 03:38.200
A read-only query can still hurt production.

55
03:38.250 --> 03:41.200
Results are capped at a thousand rows by default,

56
03:41.250 --> 03:43.200
and ten thousand at most.

57
03:43.250 --> 03:46.200
But a row cap doesn't stop a table scan,

58
03:46.250 --> 03:50.120
so a timeout cancels the statement on the IBM i itself,

59
03:50.170 --> 03:51.880
not just on the client.

60
03:51.930 --> 03:55.080
ODBC cancels on its own connection.

61
03:55.130 --> 03:58.920
JT400 and Mapepire call CANCEL_SQL,

62
03:58.970 --> 04:01.400
which needs job control authority.

63
04:01.450 --> 04:06.020
Exports have their own limits for rows, size and concurrency.

64
04:07.320 --> 04:10.370
Rate limits sit on every way in.

65
04:10.440 --> 04:15.590
Tool calls are limited per token, a hundred per fifteen minutes by default,

66
04:15.640 --> 04:18.690
and a refreshed token keeps the same budget.

67
04:18.760 --> 04:22.130
Sign-ins allow five attempts a minute per address.

68
04:22.360 --> 04:27.110
They are counted when they arrive, so parallel guesses don't slip through.

69
04:27.160 --> 04:32.530
A success doesn't reset the failures, and these limits can't be switched off.

70
04:32.600 --> 04:36.470
Behind a proxy, set MCP_TRUST_PROXY,

71
04:36.520 --> 04:39.430
or every client shares one budget.

72
04:39.480 --> 04:44.930
Underneath, the IBM i still counts failed sign-ons for each profile.

73
04:46.230 --> 04:50.840
Over HTTP, the edge is checked before any route.

74
04:50.950 --> 04:53.660
The host header must be on an allowlist,

75
04:53.710 --> 04:55.840
which stops DNS rebinding.

76
04:56.310 --> 05:02.160
Browser origins must match, and cross-origin requests never carry credentials.

77
05:02.630 --> 05:06.540
The server refuses to run without auth on a public address,

78
05:06.590 --> 05:10.760
and the public URL must use HTTPS.

79
05:10.950 --> 05:15.840
Put it behind a proxy, and allow only your clients' address ranges.

80
05:17.140 --> 05:20.270
The O Auth server is small and strict.

81
05:20.580 --> 05:25.550
PKCE is required, with S256 only.

82
05:25.780 --> 05:28.930
Redirect URIs must be on an allowlist,

83
05:28.980 --> 05:33.150
so nobody can register a client that sends codes to their own site.

84
05:33.620 --> 05:36.610
Codes work once and expire in a minute,

85
05:36.660 --> 05:40.190
and a replayed code revokes every token it issued.

86
05:40.820 --> 05:43.330
State is signed with HMAC,

87
05:43.380 --> 05:47.130
the sign-in page has a strict content security policy,

88
05:47.180 --> 05:50.430
and a failed sign-in shows a generic message.

89
05:51.730 --> 05:55.580
On the wire, turn on TLS to the host servers.

90
05:56.050 --> 06:02.020
Mapepire runs over SSH, and checks the host key before the password is sent.

91
06:02.210 --> 06:05.680
At rest, sign-ins live in memory by default,

92
06:05.730 --> 06:08.060
and a restart signs everyone out.

93
06:08.450 --> 06:12.880
To keep them, a state file is encrypted with AES-GCM,

94
06:12.930 --> 06:16.160
and stores only hashes of the refresh tokens.

95
06:16.210 --> 06:19.220
Keep the file and its secret apart.

96
06:19.410 --> 06:24.220
Profiles refuse plain-text passwords, and the logs redact them.

97
06:25.520 --> 06:28.090
Now the question that matters most.

98
06:28.160 --> 06:31.290
The rows a query returns go to the model.

99
06:31.680 --> 06:34.750
With a hosted assistant, they leave your network,

100
06:34.800 --> 06:37.170
and the provider's terms apply.

101
06:37.360 --> 06:41.070
Use an assistant your organization has approved for this data,

102
06:41.120 --> 06:44.330
or a local model inside your network.

103
06:44.560 --> 06:47.990
Keep what comes back small, with the allowlist,

104
06:48.040 --> 06:49.690
masking and row limits.

105
06:50.000 --> 06:54.330
And treat an export link like a password until it expires.

106
06:55.630 --> 06:59.260
Every tool call writes one JSON line:

107
06:59.310 --> 07:04.620
who, which tool, which system, how long, how many rows,

108
07:04.670 --> 07:06.520
and which check refused it.

109
07:06.990 --> 07:09.500
The SQL is hashed by default,

110
07:09.550 --> 07:12.960
so the log doesn't become a second copy of the data.

111
07:13.150 --> 07:16.620
Sign-ins are logged with the outcome and the address.

112
07:16.670 --> 07:19.080
Treat this log as personal data.

113
07:19.310 --> 07:24.920
Tell your users, keep it short, and think twice before shipping it to a third party.

114
07:26.220 --> 07:28.090
What it does not do.

115
07:28.140 --> 07:31.210
It does not replace object authority.

116
07:31.260 --> 07:35.450
A view in an allowed library can still read another library,

117
07:35.500 --> 07:38.810
so give each profile only what it needs.

118
07:38.860 --> 07:42.837
Unqualified functions resolve through the library list.

119
07:42.887 --> 07:47.050
And injected text in the data can still mislead an answer.

120
07:47.100 --> 07:52.470
It cannot raise the user's authority, but people should check the numbers that matter.

121
07:53.770 --> 07:56.360
Finally, the code you run.

122
07:56.410 --> 08:00.360
Releases are built and published to npm from CI,

123
08:00.410 --> 08:03.680
with trusted publishing, not from a laptop.

124
08:03.730 --> 08:06.627
Dependabot keeps dependencies current.

125
08:06.677 --> 08:09.480
The Docker image runs as a non-root user,

126
08:09.530 --> 08:12.120
and you should pin a version in production.

127
08:12.170 --> 08:13.587
Found a problem?

128
08:13.637 --> 08:15.960
Report it privately through GitHub.

129
08:16.010 --> 08:18.760
And every check in this video is open source,

130
08:18.810 --> 08:20.580
so you can read it yourself.

131
08:22.830 --> 08:26.580
Read the security guide and its production checklist.

132
08:26.630 --> 08:29.560
Then decide with the facts in front of you.
